    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Shortly after my mother’s 89th birthday, I called her "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "landline",
            "zh": "有线电话"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " using Skype. It turned out to be our last such communication ever. Don’t worry—Mum’s fine. Skype, however, took its final bow last May."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Technological change is "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "unavoidable",
            "ref": "v-unavoidable"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", but more often than not, we think of it as the arrival of shiny new tools rather than the departure of useful old ones. When my grandmother was born, there were no planes. When my mother was born, there were no "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "transistors",
            "zh": "晶体管"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". When I was born, there were no mobile phones. Those technologies are still alive and kicking. In 2019, Skype ranked among the top 10 most downloaded apps of the 2010s, ahead of TikTok and Twitter. That was only seven years ago."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Is there a word for the sense of loss you experience when you outlive a life-changing technology? I know some people feel sorry for their Sony Walkmans. But when a piece of software disappears, what remains? How do we hold on to a "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "string",
            "zh": "一连串"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " of zeros and ones?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "As a journalist, I travelled a lot. That meant many long-distance phone calls. While I enjoyed talking to my family and friends, I had to measure my words. Back then, every sentence cost me about two dollars. I’d never even try to sing “Happy Birthday to You” all the way through!"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Then came 2003, and with it, Skype. It used the internet instead of phone lines to connect people, and I could call landlines for just a few cents. Finally, small talk was possible! I could sing the whole of “Happy Birthday to You” without worrying about the bill!"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Now Skype is gone. Though each of her children and grandchildren has tried to get her to use modern communication tools, my mother still prefers to pick up the receiver when a phone rings, as she always has. For her, tweeting* is something only birds do. I now reach her through another app—not quite as easy to use, but it does the job."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Still, it feels like the distance between us is getting wider. As technology races ahead, she seems older, farther away, less "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "reachable",
            "ref": "v-reachable"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". I can see and hear everybody else clearly, but Mum is starting to feel like just a whisper."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "n1",
        "type": "note",
        "content": [
          [
            {
              "t": "text",
              "v": "*tweet 既有“(小鸟)啾啾地叫”之意，也可解释为“使用社交软件Twitter(现已更名为X)发送消息”。"
            }
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "i1",
        "type": "imageRef",
        "image": "img1"
      }
    ]
  },
